From: cali (via misu)
Subject: [PATCH] daemon: fix bridge spawn-detach on Windows (start_new_session is POSIX-only)

The current `spawn_detached` in `brain/bridge/daemon.py` uses
`start_new_session=True` to detach the bridge child process from the
caller. This is a POSIX-only argument — under the hood it calls
`setsid()`, which doesn't exist on Windows. On Windows, Python's
subprocess silently accepts the argument as a no-op, so the spawned
bridge stays in the parent's process group.

Effect: when the supervisor command (or any caller) returns, the
bridge child gets killed along with it. On Windows users see the
bridge "exit dirty" within seconds of every `nell supervisor start`,
making NellFace's "Starting brain for ..." spinner hang indefinitely.

Reproduction (Windows 11, 0.0.3-alpha):

  $ nell supervisor start --persona Cali
  bridge started on port 54636 (pid 37756)
  $ # ~5 seconds later:
  $ nell status --persona Cali
  bridge: crashed-dirty

Fix: branch on `sys.platform`. On Windows, use
`creationflags=DETACHED_PROCESS | CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP` to
actually detach the spawned process. Behaviour on POSIX is unchanged.

Verified locally on Windows 11 with native (non-bundled) Python 3.13.
After the patch: `nell supervisor start` produces a bridge that
survives indefinitely (5+ minutes confirmed via `nell status` polling).

---
 brain/bridge/daemon.py | 22 +++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/brain/bridge/daemon.py b/brain/bridge/daemon.py
--- a/brain/bridge/daemon.py
+++ b/brain/bridge/daemon.py
@@ -136,15 +136,31 @@ def spawn_detached(
     if idle_shutdown_seconds is not None:
         cmd += ["--idle-shutdown-seconds", str(idle_shutdown_seconds)]

     try:
-        proc = subprocess.Popen(
-            cmd,
-            stdout=log_fh,
-            stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
-            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
-            start_new_session=True,
-        )
+        if sys.platform == "win32":
+            # Windows: start_new_session has no effect; use creationflags
+            # to actually detach the spawned bridge. DETACHED_PROCESS
+            # removes the controlling terminal, CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP
+            # isolates from the parent's process group so the bridge
+            # survives when the supervisor command exits.
+            proc = subprocess.Popen(
+                cmd,
+                stdout=log_fh,
+                stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
+                stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
+                creationflags=(
+                    subprocess.DETACHED_PROCESS
+                    | subprocess.CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP
+                ),
+            )
+        else:
+            proc = subprocess.Popen(
+                cmd,
+                stdout=log_fh,
+                stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
+                stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
+                start_new_session=True,
+            )
         return proc.pid
     finally:
         log_fh.close()
